Figure 2 illustrates the in vivo retention and clearance kinetics of Nano-11, a dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle, after intramuscular injection in mice over a 3-week period. Whole-mouse imaging (A) shows that Alexa Fluor 647-labeled Nano-11 remains primarily localized at the injection site in the calf muscles, with minimal distribution to internal organs, confirmed by imaging excised organs on day 21 (B). Quantification of fluorescence intensity at the injection site (C) reveals an initial rapid decrease on day 1, followed by a moderate increase on day 2 (potentially due to edema), and then a steady decline, with approximately 20% of the initial signal remaining by day 21, demonstrating gradual local clearance.